Got it — `with`, `modifiers`, and `children` must each be usable as the *static entry* or *chained*, order-independently:

```java
// all three orderings equivalent
VStack.with(v -> v.spacing(8)).children(a, b).modifiers(Padding.with(p -> p.uniform(16)));
VStack.children(a, b).with(v -> v.spacing(8)).modifiers(Padding.with(p -> p.uniform(16)));
VStack.modifiers(Padding.with(p -> p.uniform(16))).children(a, b).with(v -> v.spacing(8));
```

I'll fold that into the plan (every one of the three gets a static entry form *and* a chainable form; the config builder is order-independent; a later call overrides an earlier set value for the same property).

One Java reality needs a decision before I can write an implementable spec: **Java forbids a class from declaring a static and an instance method with the same signature, and a static method cannot hide an inherited instance method of the same signature.** So `Text.with(Consumer<Config>)` static and `view.with(Consumer<Config>)` instance cannot coexist verbatim under one name.

